The DEAD-SIM mutation — the red proof for criterion 10.12's completion predicate.

`advance()`'s loop is neutered so no tick ever runs. The function keeps its signature and its
return type, so `tsc` is happy and `vite build` succeeds: the RED has to come from the spec, which
is the whole point. The game still boots, still draws, and the courier's idle sheet still loops at
7.5 fps with `repeat: -1` — so "pixels changed" stays TRUE under this mutation and only the
completion transition can see it.

It is a committed fixture rather than a throwaway edit, matching `bad-docs/`, `bad-levels/`,
`bad-sim/` and `bad-style/`: a red proof nobody can re-run is a claim, not evidence *(vault C2)*.
It is deliberately NOT a runtime flag — a production mutation selector would itself be a new dev
seam in the very bundle this phase exists to prove clean.

    git apply --ignore-whitespace tests/fixtures/dead-sim.patch && npm run build
    npx playwright test --project=chromium-prod -g "boots, draws"
    git apply -R --ignore-whitespace tests/fixtures/dead-sim.patch && npm run build
    npx playwright test --project=chromium-prod -g "boots, draws"

🔴 **READ THE COUNTS, NOT THE EXIT CODE.** `--project` plus `-g` is exactly the shape that selects
NOTHING: Playwright then prints `expected: 0, unexpected: 0` and **exits 0**, indistinguishable from
a clean pass unless the count is read (CLAUDE.md §5). The reverse-apply step is the dangerous
direction — a zero-selection green reads as *"the mutation reverted cleanly"*. So:

    mutated  → must print `1 failed`   (not `0 failed`, not `expected: 0`)
    reverted → must print `1 passed`

⚠️ **`npm run build` is not optional in either line.** `prod-server.mjs` serves whatever `dist/`
happens to be on disk and refuses only if `dist/index.html` is MISSING, never if it is STALE. Run
the bare `npx playwright test` without rebuilding and you test the previous build — a green that
proves the mutation was never compiled.

Git ignores everything above the first `diff --git` line.
